/usr/bin/change from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for change, so this is the disassembly, and change.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f change.ctl change.dist

change.dist is the binary off inode 63 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 11757
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It is a Whitesmith a.out - 99 94, text
at 0100 for 244f bytes and data at 3000 - in the older runtime that login, td
and include have: c.ent at 24f4 and c.ent2 at 24ff, saving the register cells at
3988, 398a and 398c.  It is the program of that name in Kernighan and Plauger's
Software Tools, with a pattern language that is edit's - the manual says "exactly
as in edit" - and with tags, which Software Tools' has not.

THE SHAPE

Its own code is the first twenty functions and a main at 084f, and then the
library from 13ef.  The pattern is a list of nodes of eight bytes - the next
node, a type, a pointer to a set, a closure flag and a character - which main
makes with makpat (0b08), and a replacement string made with makesub (0f84) in
which "&" is 0366 and a tag reference 0377 down to 0367.  Then, for each line,
amatch (10fc) is tried at every place in turn and catsub (0111) makes what
replaces a match; Software Tools' own, K&P's, except where it says otherwise.

change.c follows that closely enough that the names and the order are the
binary's, and that its quirks are.  The ones that matter:

  - The node types are 'a' for a character, '?' for any but the newline, '['
    for a set and ']' for a set that is the opposite - which does not match
    the newline either - '%' and '$' for the ends of a line, and '{' and '}' for
    a tag's start and end.  A closure is a flag on the node before the *, and a
    * after a % is a * of its own, as one at the very start is; a * after
    anything else sets the flag, "$*" and the tags' too, so that "$*" is a pattern
    that matches the empty string everywhere.

  - "%" and "$" mean what they say wherever they are in the pattern.  The end of
    a line is the newline: "$" matches it, and is the one character that
    matches and is not in what the match includes - the match is said to end at
    the last character of the string when it ends at the string's end, so
    that the newline stays out of the text that is replaced.

  - A tag is "@(" to "@)" and a tag reference "@1" to "@9", numbered by the "@("
    from the left.  A tenth is "too many tagged fields.", an "@)" with no "@(" is
    "imbalanced parentheses.", and an "@(" that is not closed is not an error.
    "@c" is c, for any other c, in the pattern and in the replacement, and so is
    "@" at the end.  The tags of a failed attempt are still there at the next
    place tried in the same line - they are cleared at each place, not
    within it.

  - A set is "[" to the first "]", with a-b for the characters between, a - at
    the start or at the end taken as itself, "@c" as c, and "^" at the start for
    the complement.  A "]" first is the end of an empty set, which matches
    nothing.

  - Whatever the cause, "illegal from pattern." and "illegal to pattern." are what
    it says when makpat or makesub returns -3.  They return the address they
    stopped at, and 0177775 is an address in the arguments' area: so "change ?
    ''" is an error or not by the length of the arguments before and after it.
    change.c has the same test, and the arguments are laid out the same way,
    so it is the same.  Errors and the usage line go to the standard output.

THE ORIGINAL'S OWN FAULTS

A pattern with a character class in it of exactly three characters - "[a-c]",
"[abc]", "[x-z]" - makes the original hang, and one with two classes in it makes
it stop quietly with nothing written, or write garbage.  It is the library's
allocator: the set is a malloc of the size of the set and its end, and a block of
four bytes is more than the free list can take.  It is not change's, and
change.c does not do it.

A "[" with no "]" after it is walked off the end of the argument and into the
next one: "change [a X" makes the pattern "[aX", and "change [abc" has a pattern
that is whatever follows it in memory.  change.c walks the same way.

A line that comes out of the changes of more than 511 characters ran over the
end of out - the next thing is the pattern's head - and then nothing is
predictable.  change.c's buffer is 4096.

The line is read as fgets reads it, 511 characters at a time, and the last line
of a file with no newline at the end of it is not looked at at all: it is read,
the end of the file is met, and the test is made after.

The exit status was 0 for an error and whatever was in BC for a good run.  It
is 1 and 0.

HOW IT WAS CHECKED

In usersim against the original, byte for byte, with both programs run under the
same name in different roots so that the arguments are at the same addresses:
five thousand random cases - patterns made of characters, ?, *, %, $, @c, sets,
ranges, tags and closures, one set at most and none of three, a replacement
of &, @1 to @9, @@, @c and text, and text of up to thirty characters in five
lines - with no difference; the usage line; every error; a line of 100 to 1100
characters; a file with no newline at its end; bytes over 0177; and an empty
file.  The eleven that are not the same are the ones described above.
